Recent statements from OpenAI and Anthropic—amid the series of unsanctioned agent incidents this summer—point to growing reliance on embedded evaluators as an additional line of defense against risks from AI systems. While welcome in principle, we must judge these arrangements by their substance, not their mere presence, to assess their potential to meaningfully reduce risk.

Crucially, these embedded evaluation deals are currently voluntary, as no state legislation formalizing third-party AI audits has taken effect yet, and Congress is still considering proposals for a federal framework. Meanwhile, AI capabilities show no sign of slowing, while progress on managing and mitigating risks lags behind. In this context, embedded evaluation agreements signed now must proactively account for the risk of producing "safety theater" and ensure evaluators’ work is credible, robust, and meaningful.

In light of this, my new @iapsAI policy memo, out today, offers five contractual foundations that these voluntary arrangements should be built on:

1️⃣ Ensure embedded evaluation arrangements can operate across the model lifecycle;
2️⃣ Let embedded evaluators choose the objects of their assessment;
3️⃣ Give embedded evaluators sufficient time to do their work;
4️⃣ Proactively reduce conflicts of interest; and
5️⃣ Guarantee non-retaliation for good-faith whistleblowing.

Read more in the linked post.

